# Day 6 - Dockerfile Basics

## Commands Learned

mkdir myapp
cd myapp

nano index.html
→ Create HTML file

nano Dockerfile
→ Create Dockerfile

docker build -t myapp:v1 .
→ Build Docker image

docker run -d -p 8081:80 myapp:v1
→ Run container from custom image

docker images
→ List all images

docker ps
→ Show running containers

## Key Concepts

Dockerfile = A file containing instructions to build a Docker image

Image = Blueprint of application

Container = Running instance of image

Build ≠ Run
→ Building an image does not automatically update running containers

## Dockerfile Example

FROM nginx

COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html

## Explanation

FROM nginx
→ Uses nginx as base image

COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html
→ Copies local file into container's web directory

## Observations

* Changes in HTML file are not reflected automatically
* Need to rebuild image after changes
* Running container does not update automatically when image changes
* Multiple versions of images can run on different ports

## Versioning Concept

myapp:v1 → Old version
myapp:v2 → Updated version

Each version creates a separate container

## Debugging Learned

* If page not loading → check container using docker ps
* If wrong content → check which container/port is running
* If changes not visible → rebuild image
* If port not working → check port conflicts

## What I Understood

* Dockerfile is used to create custom images
* Containers are isolated and do not update automatically
* Image changes require new container deployment
* Port mapping connects host and container

## Real Life Use

* Developers create Dockerfile for applications
* CI/CD builds images from Dockerfile
* Servers run containers using these images
* New versions are deployed by creating new containers
